\title{#{Δ¹}-Étale maps}
\author{fredrikbakke}
\date{2026-09-19}
\taxon{Module}
\import{stt-macros}

\p{
Assume a [tiny](stt-00JY) interval. Then a crisp map is [étale](bak-922B) for
[#{Δ¹}-discrete types](stt-00B6) exactly when it is a
[Kan fibration](bak-8ED4), i.e. a left and a right fibration.
}

\agda{
open import Foundations.Universes
open import Algebra.Lattice

module Synthetic.Categories.Δ¹EtaleMaps
  {@♭ 𝓘} (@♭ Δ¹ : Type 𝓘) (@♭ I : Lattice Δ¹)
  where

open Lattice I renaming (0l to i0; 1l to i1)
open import Axioms.UF
}

\agda-imports{
\agda{
open import Foundations.Prelude
open import Core.GlobalClassesMaps
open import Core.Orthogonal
open import Modalities.EtaleMaps
open import Modalities.GlobalReflectiveSubuniverses.DescentEtale
open import Modalities.ModalEquivalences
open import Modalities.Instances.Nullification
open import Modalities.Instances.Truncation
open import Synthetic.Tiny
open import Synthetic.Categories.Groupoids Δ¹ I
open import Synthetic.Categories.CovariantFamilies Δ¹ I
open import Synthetic.Categories.KanFibrations Δ¹ I
}
}

\subtree[bak-12BF]{
\title{#{Δ¹}-Étale maps are Kan fibrations}
\taxon{Lemma}
\author{fredrikbakke}
\date{2026-09-19}

\p{
Point inclusions of the interval are #{Δ¹}-equivalences, so
every #{Δ¹}-étale map is a Kan fibration.
}

\agda{
opaque
  point-is-Δ¹-modal-equiv
    : (i : Δ¹) → is-modal-equiv Δ¹DiscreteType-gRSubU (point i)
  point-is-Δ¹-modal-equiv i
    = section-is-modal-equiv Δ¹DiscreteType-gRSubU (point i) ! ~refl
        (is-modal-equiv←up-modal-equiv Δ¹DiscreteType-gRSubU
          (λ disc → is-local←is-null disc tt))

module _ {𝓤 𝓥} {E : Type 𝓤} {B : Type 𝓥} {f : E → B} where

  point-lifting←is-Δ¹-étale
    : (i : Δ¹) → is-étale-map Δ¹DiscreteType-gRSubU f
    → are-orthogonal (point i) f
  point-lifting←is-Δ¹-étale i
    = modal-equiv⊥etale Δ¹DiscreteType-gRSubU (point-is-Δ¹-modal-equiv i)

  is-kan-fibration←is-Δ¹-étale
    : is-étale-map Δ¹DiscreteType-gRSubU f → is-kan-fibration f
  is-kan-fibration←is-Δ¹-étale et b
    = point-lifting←is-Δ¹-étale (fam-generators-kan b tt) et
}
}

\agda{
module TinyInterval
  (@♭ √ : ∀ {@♭ 𝓤} → @♭ Type 𝓤 → Type 𝓤)
  (@♭ √ε : ∀ {@♭ 𝓤} {@♭ A : Type 𝓤} → (Δ¹ → √ A) → A)
  (@♭ √-is-tiny-str
    : ∀ {@♭ 𝓤} {@♭ A : Type 𝓤} → is-tiny-structure Δ¹ A (√ A) √ε)
  (open Dependent√ Δ¹ √ √ε √-is-tiny-str using (√ᵈ))
  (@♭ √ᵈ-is-prop
    : ∀ {@♭ 𝓤 𝓥} {@♭ Γ : Type 𝓤} (@♭ A : (Δ¹ → Γ) → Type 𝓥)
      → @♭ ((γ : Δ¹ → Γ) → is-prop (A γ)) → (γ : Γ) → is-prop (√ᵈ A γ))
  (let module Classifier
        = AmazinglyFibrant
            Δ¹ √ √ε √-is-tiny-str
            is-kan is-kan-is-prop is-kan-is-Δ¹-relative)
  where
}

\subtree[bak-1FB1]{
\title{The universal Kan fibration}
\taxon{Construction}
\author{fredrikbakke}
\date{2026-09-24}

\p{
The [amazing fibrancy construction](stt-00KS) gives a universal Kan fibration
#{\mathrm{Kan}^* \to \mathrm{Kan}}. Its codomain classifies crisp Kan families,
and its domain consists of a classified type together with a point.
}

\agda{
  open Classifier public
    renaming
      ( is-afib to is-akan
      ; UFib to Kan
      ; mk-ufib to mk-kan
      ; UFib∙ to Kan∗
      ; UFib-π to Kan-π
      ; UFib-π-is-fib to Kan-π-is-kan
      ; UFib-is-fib to Kan-family-is-kan
      ; UFib-transport to Kan-family
      ; liftFib to liftKan
      ; lift-fibrancy to lift-kan-fibrancy)

  open Classifier.UIdS √ᵈ-is-prop public
    renaming
      ( is-afib-is-prop to is-akan-is-prop
      ; UFib-path← to Kan-path←
      ; UFib-path←-coe≃-ap to Kan-path←-coe≃-ap
      ; UFib-path←-β to Kan-path←-β
      ; UFib-IdS to Kan-IdS
      ; UFib-tr to Kan-tr
      ; UP-UFib to UP-Kan
      ; UP-UFib≃ to UP-Kan≃)

  open Kan public
}
}

\subtree[bak-0D64]{
\title{The Kan fibration classifier is #{Δ¹}-discrete}
\taxon{Theorem}
\author{fredrikbakke}
\date{2026-09-19}

\p{
Any synthetic arrow in the Kan fibration classifier gives an equivalence by
transport and hence an identification, and we know a family of maps
#{\Hom(x,y)\to (x = y)}
[implies that the underlying type is #{Δ¹}-discrete](stt-00L4).
}

\agda{
  Kan-is-Δ¹-discrete
    : ∀ {@♭ 𝓤} → is-Δ¹-discrete (Kan 𝓤)
  Kan-is-Δ¹-discrete
    = is-Δ¹-discrete←Id←Hom
        (λ {a} {b} → Kan-path← a b ∘ tr-hom≃←is-kan Kan-π-is-kan)

  Kan-π-fibres-are-Δ¹-discrete
    : ∀ {@♭ 𝓤} (a : Kan 𝓤) → is-Δ¹-discrete (Carrier a)
  Kan-π-fibres-are-Δ¹-discrete
    = is-Δ¹-discrete-family←is-covariant
        (is-covariant←is-kan Carrier Kan-π-is-kan)
}
}

\subtree[bak-99D7]{
\title{Characterisation of #{Δ¹}-étale maps}
\taxon{Theorem}
\author{fredrikbakke}
\date{2026-09-19}

\p{
A crisp map #{f \colon-flat E \to B} is #{\Delta^1}-étale if and only if it is a Kan
fibration.
}

\proof{
\p{
The forwards direction was demonstrated in [[bak-12BF]]. For the converse, let #{f}
be a crisp map whose fibre family is Kan. The universal Kan family has
#{Δ¹}-discrete base and fibres, and every crisp Kan family is classified by it,
so the [classification by a modal family criterion](bak-4877) gives that #{f} is
étale.
}
}

\agda{
  module _ {@♭ 𝓤 𝓥} {@♭ E : Type 𝓤} {@♭ B : Type 𝓥} (@♭ f : E → B) where

    is-Δ¹-étale←fibres-are-kan
      : is-kan (fibre f) → is-étale-map Δ¹DiscreteType-gRSubU f
    is-Δ¹-étale←fibres-are-kan bi
      = is-étale-map←classified-by-modal-family
          Δ¹DiscreteType-gRSubU
          Σ-is-Δ¹-discrete
          Carrier
          (λ b → mk-kan (fibre f b) (lift-kan-fibrancy (Trunc.η i0) (fibre f) bi b))
          Kan-is-Δ¹-discrete
          Kan-π-fibres-are-Δ¹-discrete
          (λ _ → id≃)

    is-Δ¹-étale↔fibres-are-kan
      : is-étale-map Δ¹DiscreteType-gRSubU f ↔ is-kan (fibre f)
    is-Δ¹-étale↔fibres-are-kan .fst
      = is-kan-fibre↔is-kan-fibration f .snd ∘ is-kan-fibration←is-Δ¹-étale
    is-Δ¹-étale↔fibres-are-kan .snd
      = is-Δ¹-étale←fibres-are-kan

    is-Δ¹-étale↔is-kan-fibration
      : is-étale-map Δ¹DiscreteType-gRSubU f ↔ is-kan-fibration f
    is-Δ¹-étale↔is-kan-fibration .fst = is-kan-fibration←is-Δ¹-étale
    is-Δ¹-étale↔is-kan-fibration .snd
      = is-Δ¹-étale←fibres-are-kan ∘ is-kan-fibre↔is-kan-fibration f .snd
}
}

\subtree[bak-980E]{
\title{Crisp Kan fibrations lift against every point inclusion of Δ¹}
\taxon{Corollary}
\author{fredrikbakke}
\date{2026-09-24}

\p{
If the interval is tiny, then any crisp Kan fibration #{f \colon-flat A \to B} lifts
against every point inclusion of the interval. In particular, we do not need to
assume the end points cover the global points, or any of the other axioms of
Triangulated Type Theory \citek{GWB24}.
}

\agda{
  module _ {@♭ 𝓤 𝓥} {@♭ E : Type 𝓤} {@♭ B : Type 𝓥} (@♭ f : E → B) where

    point-lifting←is-kan-fibration
      : is-kan-fibration f
      → (i : Δ¹) → are-orthogonal (point i) f
    point-lifting←is-kan-fibration kan i
      = point-lifting←is-Δ¹-étale i
          (is-Δ¹-étale↔is-kan-fibration f .snd kan)
}
}
